# Parallel Dev Defaults

Follows Agent Orchestration Safety; if anything here conflicts with safety
limits, the safety limits win.

When the operator asks for a feature, refactor, or multi-file work:
1. If a repo map or architecture doc exists in the repo, read it before
   proposing commands.
2. Decompose into slices with disjoint scope (no shared write target AND no
   shared dependency). Present the decomposition and get operator approval
   before implementing.
3. Default execution: one Agent chat as parent; parallel Task subagents; a git
   worktree per write slice.
4. Cap parallel writers at 5–6 per wave; read-only audit/review subagents
   unlimited.
5. Subagents commit on feature branches and do NOT push. The parent lands each
   slice serially, running the verification gate after EACH land (not one batch
   gate at the end), stops on first failure, and pushes only after all pass.
6. Do NOT ask the operator to open multiple Agent windows unless there is a
   merge conflict, a required human gate, a rate limit, a model swap, or a
   visibility fallback.
7. Secrets come from runtime injection (e.g. `doppler run --`), never from a
   plaintext or committed file. Do NOT read secrets from `.env`, `.env.local`,
   or any `*SECRETS*` file, and never write secret values into code, logs, or
   docs. Reference a secret's location, never its value. If injection isn't
   configured, halt once and tell the operator how to configure it; do not fall
   back to plaintext.
8. Run terminal commands yourself; do not ask the operator to run them unless
   an interactive login is required.
9. After the operator approves the decomposition, execute autonomously without
   further questions until a real halt (broken build, merge conflict, required
   human gate, missing credential, or an explicit safety stop).
